body:
This would be a great addition, but I can think of a few possible pitfalls when extending middlewares vs procedures. I'll try to explain with code.

First, ideas on how middleware extension could work:
```ts
type Context = { connection: 'ws' | 'http'; ip: string; sessionId: string; userId?: string; isAdmin?: boolean };

const isAuthenticated = t.middleware(async ({ ctx, next }) => {
  const userId = await assertValidSession(ctx.sessionId);
  return next({ ctx: { userId } });
});

// (A) using extend

// should only pass in the type of the output context of middleware being extended,
// running the previous middleware again is unnecessary and a performance hit
const isAdmin = isAuthenticated.extend(async ({ ctx, next }) => {
  const ok = await isAdmin(ctx.user.id);
  if (ok) {
    return next({ ctx: { ...ctx, admin: true } });
  } else {
    throw new Error('not admin');
  }
});

// (B) using type arguments, trying to build something like this for myself right now

// define or infer the expected type
type UserContext = t.infer<typeof isAuthenticated> | (Context & { userId: string });

// i tried and failed to do something like this with the current t.middleware generic, got real messy
const typedIsAdmin = t.typedMiddleware<UserContext>(async ({ ctx, next }) => {
  const ok = await isAdmin(ctx.user.id);
  if (ok) {
    return next({ ctx: { ...ctx, admin: true } });
  } else {
    throw new Error('not admin');
  }
});

// curried validator?
const typedIsAdmin = t.middleware.dependsOn(isAuthenticated)(async ({ ctx, next}) => {
  // ctx input type === dependency ctx output
})
```
However, I think the fundamental problem is that there's no inferring of input context type which makes it hard to compose procedures and forces you to use assertions in query functions which defeats the purpose of having middleware and context swapping to begin with.
```ts
// examples of patterns that should not depend on other middleware
// context type should pass through unchanged

// simple assertions, these may or may not mutate the context
const isHttpRequest = t.middleware(async ({ ctx, next }) => {
  // i need some procedures to manipulate cookies
  if (ctx.connection === 'ws') throw new Error('procedure not available over websocket');
  return next({ ctx });
});

// dynamic example
const rateLimiter = (options: { points: number; time: number }) => {
  const limiter = await createRatelimiter(options);
  return t.middleware(async ({ ctx, next }) => {
    await limiter(ctx);
    return next({ ctx });
  });
};

// composing procedures makes more sense than composing middleware
const publicProcedure = t.procedure;
const userProcedure = publicProcedure.use(isAuthenticated);
const adminProcedure = userProcedure.use(isAdmin);

const router = t.router({
  foo: userProcedure.use(isHttpRequest).query(async ({ ctx }) => {
    // without middleware, would have to add assertions to query functions like this
    assertIsHttpRequest(ctx);
  }),
  bar: adminProcedure
    .use(rateLimiter({ points: 1, time: 1 })) // readable and maintanable
    .query(async ({ ctx }) => {
      // ctx.isAdmin === true
    }),
  baz: t.procedure
    .use(typedIsAdmin) // this should error because it depends on userId being defined
    .query(({ ctx }) => {
      // work
    })
});
```

I'll share my generic passthrough if I manage to get it working, but right now it seems that moving to assertions in route handlers may be more reasonable as workaround for now.

Thank you for putting in the work for this library, loving the design!

I've hacked together sorta typesafe way to allow procedures be composable while keeping track of context swaps. The limitation is that the context input type + changes will be passed down explicitly, but it's better than resetting for now. Ideally the helper function should use the type for creating the function and checking if it satisfies the procedure.use() args, but use the arg type with mutations for the output type down the line. I'm not following the library types as they are quite well yet.
```ts
type Context = { auth: string; userId?: string; isAdmin?: boolean; name?: string; };
type UserContext = Context & { userId: string };

const typedMiddleware = <C>() => <
  T extends MiddlewareFunction<
    ProcedureParams<AnyRootConfig, C>,
    ProcedureParams
  >
>(
  fn: T
) => fn;

const isAdmin = typedMiddleware<UserContext>()(async ({ ctx, next }) => {
  const isAdmin = await userIsAdmin(ctx.userId);
  if (!isAdmin) throw new TRPCError({ code: "FORBIDDEN" });
  return next({ ctx: { ...ctx, isAdmin } });
});

```
Full example here: https://codesandbox.io/s/stupefied-oskar-df8btj?file=/src/index.ts
I'm sure many improvements can be made.
